  <!-- Routes: the names and notes Core carries in English, said in this language. -->
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.Pcsx2">PCSX2</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.Rpcs3">RPCS3</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.Dx11">D3D11 game</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.Dx12">D3D12 game</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.Vulkan">Vulkan game</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.OpenGL">OpenGL game</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.X86Dx11">D3D11 game, 32-bit</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.X86Dx9">D3D9 game, 32-bit</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.X86Dx8">D3D8 game, 32-bit</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Preset.OptiScaler">OptiScaler: D3D12 game with DLSS, FSR or XeSS</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.Pcsx2">Set the emulator to Direct3D 11: it is the renderer where the network gets the most — the emulator’s depth, and motion from the companion effect when it is installed. Watch for a per-game override, which beats the global setting silently.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.Rpcs3">Experimental. ReShade on Vulkan is a global layer, not a proxy DLL: run its installer against rpcs3.exe and pick Vulkan. No depth on Vulkan, only colour and estimated motion.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.Dx11">The best route: D3D11 is the only one where the game’s own motion vectors reach the network, together with its depth.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.Dx12">Degraded. On D3D12 the add-on finds the game’s depth but not its motion vectors, so the network gets colour and depth and estimates the motion. It works; expect less from it. A game with DLSS, FSR or XeSS in its settings is better served by the OptiScaler route.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.OptiScaler">Recommended for D3D12 games. Instead of ReShade this installs OptiScaler, which takes over the game's DLSS, FSR or XeSS and runs the network inside it, with the game's own depth and motion vectors. The game has to offer one of those upscalers, switched on in its settings. In game, press Insert, open the Neural tab and turn on Enable NR; a game with Ray Reconstruction needs "After the finished frame" as the processing point.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.Vulkan">Experimental. ReShade on Vulkan is a global layer: run its installer against the game’s .exe and pick Vulkan. The game must import vkCreateDevice statically, and there is no depth on Vulkan.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.OpenGL">Experimental. Unlike Vulkan this is an ordinary proxy DLL: ReShade goes in as opengl32.dll beside the game, with no separate installer run. Colour and estimated motion only; the game’s own depth is reachable on this API but is not wired up yet. A 32-bit OpenGL game has no route.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.X86Dx11">Experimental. A 32-bit game cannot load the 64-bit runtime, so the add-on runs as a pair sharing frames on one adapter. Install ReShade with add-on support as the 32-bit dxgi.dll.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.X86Dx9">Experimental. The same 32-bit pair as D3D11, through a private D3D9/D3D11 stage. Plain D3D9 costs a fixed few milliseconds per frame; D3D9Ex does not. Install ReShade as the 32-bit d3d9.dll.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PresetNote.X86Dx8">Experimental. D3D8 is translated to D3D9 and then takes the D3D9 route above, with the same 32-bit pair. Install ReShade as the 32-bit d3d9.dll.</sys:String>

  <!-- First run -->
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupTitle">Getting started</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupWelcome">Welcome</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupWelcomeBody">The DLSS 5 Neural Rendering network on Radeon cards, through ReShade or OptiScaler. Four quick steps.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupStepLanguage">Language</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupStepMachine">This machine</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupStepFiles">Files</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupStepGames">Your games</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupBack">Back</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupNext">Continue</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFinish">Start using it</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupSkip">Skip for now</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupStepOf">Step {0} of {1}</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupMachineBody">Two things stop the add-on from running, and both are visible from here. Neither one blocks you from continuing — only from it working inside the game.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupMachineOk">This machine has everything the add-on needs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupMachineBad">Something is missing. You can continue and fix it later, but it will not work in game until you do.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesBody">The same files serve every game: downloaded once, checked against a pinned hash, and reused.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesCheck">Checking what is already here…</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesAllReady">Every file is here and verified.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesMissing">{0} of {1} components are still missing.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesDownload">Download everything now</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesRetry">Try again</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesLater">Skip this and they are downloaded on the first install.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupFilesOffline">Could not read the download list — most likely no connection. You can continue: the app tries again when you install.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupGamesBody">Reading the launchers is read-only: nothing is installed or changed by looking. You can add folders by hand afterwards.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupGamesApi">Each game is read to work out which graphics API it uses, and that reading is usually right but not always: one folder can hold a D3D11 and a D3D12 executable, and a game can default to one API while its executable also supports an older one. Before installing, it is worth checking the game on pcgamingwiki.com — every game card has a link straight to it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupGamesNone">No games found. You can add a folder by hand in the main window.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupGamesFound">{0} games found.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SetupDoneBody">All set. The main window opens with your library.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Unexpected">Something unexpected happened. The full log was written.</sys:String>


  <!-- Play and emulators -->
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Play">Play</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Launch">Open</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PlayFailed">Could not start it. Use Open the folder and run the executable by hand.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.AddEmulator">Add Emulator</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorTitle">Add an emulator</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorBody">Pick which one, then point at the folder it runs from — the folder with the executable, not the one with the ROMs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorOther">Another emulator</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorOtherBody">Point at the folder and I will say what I can work out from it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorPick">Point at the {0} folder</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorPickOther">Point at the emulator's folder</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorFound">{0} found: {1}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorWrong">That does not look like {0}: no {1} in that folder. Added anyway — check the path.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorUnknown">No emulator I know is in there. Added from what the files say: {0}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.EmulatorSetting">Inside the emulator</sys:String>


  <!-- Problem report -->
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ReportTitle">Not working in game?</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ReportBody">Collects the logs, what is in the game folder, and what ReShade wrote, into one .zip. Nothing is sent: the file stays on your PC for you to share where you like.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Report">Report a problem</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ReportSaved">Report saved and the folder opened: {0}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ReportFailed">The report could not be built. The logs are in {0}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ReportWorking">Collecting…</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DownloadReportTitle">Files will not download?</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DownloadReportBody">Collects what every download tried — each address, its name lookup, proxy and answer — and checks each address again now, into one .zip. Nothing is sent: the file stays on your PC for you to share where you like.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DownloadReport">Download logs</sys:String>

  <!-- The sheet's two routes, the files panel, updates. -->
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Add">Add</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.AddMenuMany">Search a folder for games…</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.AddMenuOne">One game's folder…</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.AddMenuEmulator">An emulator…</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.UpdateShort">Update</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.GamesAway">{0} on a drive that is not connected</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.GamesAwayHint">Games on a drive that is not plugged in right now stay remembered, with everything chosen for them, and come back when the drive does.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.GameGone">{0} is no longer installed, so it left the list.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.GamesGone">{0} game is no longer installed, so it left the list.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.GamesGone.Many">{0} games are no longer installed, so they left the list.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ShowDetails">Show the details</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DetectedSection">Detected</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.CheckApiTip">Files say what a game links against, not what it runs on: one folder can hold a D3D11 and a D3D12 executable. The wiki says which the game uses.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DetectedFromFiles">Read from the game's own files.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DetectedFromWiki">From PCGamingWiki and the game's files.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.InstallWith">Install with</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteReShade">ReShade + add-on</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteOpti">OptiScaler</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Recommended">Recommended</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RecommendedLower">recommended</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteReShadeSummary">The add-on inside ReShade. D3D11, D3D12, Vulkan, OpenGL, 32-bit games and emulators.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteReShadeMisfit">No ReShade route for what this game reads as ({0}).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteOptiSummary">Runs inside the game's DLSS, FSR or XeSS, with its own depth and motion. Replaces ReShade.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteOptiFound">Runs inside the game's upscaler ({0} found here). Replaces ReShade.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.RouteOptiMisfit">Made for 64-bit D3D12 games; this one reads as {0}.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ApiSection">Graphics API</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Group64">64-bit games</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Group32">32-bit games (experimental)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.GroupEmulators">Emulators</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.OptiAbout">Instead of ReShade this installs OptiScaler, which takes over the game's DLSS, FSR or XeSS and runs the network inside it, with the game's own depth and motion vectors.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.InGame">In the game</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.OptiStep1">Turn on DLSS, FSR or XeSS in the game's graphics settings.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.OptiStep2">Press Insert, open the Neural tab and turn on Enable NR.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.OptiStep3">With Ray Reconstruction on, set the processing point to "After the finished frame".</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.InstallOpti">Install OptiScaler</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Update">Update</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.Reinstall">Reinstall</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchToOpti">Switch to OptiScaler</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchToReShade">Switch to ReShade</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchTitle">Switch to {0}?</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchBody">{0} has the {1} route installed, and the two cannot be in one folder at once. {1} is removed first, putting back anything it replaced, and then {2} is installed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchYes">Remove {0}, install {1}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchYesBody">Your own settings files stay where they are.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.SwitchNoBody">Nothing is changed; {0} stays installed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DownloadFailedDetail">Why is under Details. Nothing was installed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DownloadFailedHelp">The files can also be fetched with a browser and handed over: This machine &gt; Files &gt; Download manually, then Import files. Files in your Downloads folder are found on their own.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DnsRetry">Clear DNS and try again</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DnsFlushFailed">Windows would not clear its DNS cache from here. Open a Command Prompt, run ipconfig /flushdns, and try again.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PreflightErr.Many">{0} problems found in this folder</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PreflightWarn.Many">Ready to install, with {0} notes to read</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.InstallWarn.Many">Installed on {0}, with {1} warnings to read below.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.UninstallWarn.Many">Removed from {0}, with {1} warnings to read below.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ResultErrors.Many">{0} problems listed below.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ManifestFailedShort">No download list</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.FailedShort">Failed</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PayloadsAllReadyShort">All {0} ready</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.PayloadsMissingShort">{0} missing · {1}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.DownloadFailedOne">Could not download: {0}</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ManualDownload">Download manually</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ImportFiles">Import files…</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ImportHint">Pick a folder with files you downloaded yourself. Each one is found by its name and taken only if it matches its pinned hash.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ManualTitle">Download the files yourself</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ManualBody">Open each link in a browser and save the file anywhere: the Downloads folder is searched on its own, and Import files takes any other folder. Names do not need changing, and nothing that does not match its pinned SHA-256 is used.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ImportPick">Pick the folder with the files you downloaded</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ImportAll">Every file is here now and verified.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ImportNothing">Nothing in that folder matched a file this needs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Str.ImportSome">Taken: {0}. Still missing: {1}.</sys:String>
